I have some tables where updates occur in the meta object, but not always in the data itself. This resulted in stale meta values.

In the following MWE, I expect the resulting cell's render to match the contents of the textInput, but it only gets the first value.

```r
library(shiny)
library(reactable)

ui <- list(
  textInput(inputId = "value", label = "Enter value: ", value = "foo"),
  reactableOutput("table")
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {
  output$table <- renderReactable({
    reactable(
      data.frame(z = 1),
      columns = list(
        z = colDef(
          cell = htmlwidgets::JS("
            function(cellInfo, state) {
              return state.meta.value;
            }
          ")
        )
      ),
      meta = list(value = input$value)
    )
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```

If I change the data frame contents, however, the meta value is properly updated:

```r
library(shiny)
library(reactable)

ui <- list(
  textInput(inputId = "value", label = "Enter value: ", value = "foo"),
  reactableOutput("table")
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {
  count <- reactiveVal(0)
  output$table <- renderReactable({
    count(isolate(count()) + 1)
    reactable(
      data.frame(z = isolate(count())),
      columns = list(
        z = colDef(
          cell = htmlwidgets::JS("
            function(cellInfo, state) {
              return state.meta.value;
            }
          ")
        )
      ),
      meta = list(value = input$value)
    )
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```

Hello, thanks for this awesome package! I'm doing a lot of work with list-cols and struct-cols with custom `cell` and `aggregate` render functions. It's all working great, except when I try to sort by these columns.

To remedy this, what would be really nice is the ability to have `sortCell` and `sortAggregated` options in my `colDef` as Javascript comparator functions. For example, the following would sort a list-col by the number of elements in the column:

```r
df <- tibble(
  a = list(
    list(1, 2, 3),
    list(1),
    list(1, 2, 3, 4),
    list(1, 2),
    list(2),
  )
)

reactable(
  df,
  columns = list(
    a = colDef(
      sortCell = JS("
        function(a, b) {
          return a.length - b.length;
        }
      ")
    )
  )
)
```

Similarly, the following would sort a struct-col by the "sortkey" property:

```r
df <- tibble(
  a = list(
    list(sortkey = 1, foo = "hello"),
    list(sortkey = 10, foo = "world"),
    list(sortkey = 2, foo = "foo"),
    list(sortkey = 10, foo = "bar"),
    list(sortkey = 8, foo = "baz")
  )
)

reactable(
  df,
  columns = list(
    a = colDef(
      sortCell = JS("
        function(a, b) {
          return a.sortkey - b.sortkey;
        },
      ")
      cell = JS("
        function(cellInfo) {
          return cellInfo.foo;
        }
      ")
    )
  )
)
```

`sortAggregated` would work exactly the same way, but apply to aggregated cells.

Without `sortCell` and `sortAggregated` I am resorting to populating my reactable with my desired sortkeys, storing all my actual data in the `.meta` object, and then doing lookups in my `cell` and `aggregated` renderers... Quite a headache, as you can imagine!

Anyway, thanks again for the awesome pkg, and please let me know if there are any other approaches to do this I've overlooked!

I just noticed this morning that when using `striped = TRUE`, `reactableTheme(stripedColor = "")` and sticky columns, `stripedColor` is not respected. 

```r
reactable::reactable(
    mtcars,
    theme = reactable::reactableTheme(
        color = "white",
        backgroundColor = "#1d1f21",
        stripedColor = "#4a4b4d"
    ),
    columns = list(
        carb = reactable::colDef(
            sticky = "right"
        )
    ),
    striped = TRUE
)
```

Inspecting the css,

```css
.rt-tr-striped-sticky {
    background-color: #f7f7f7;
}
```

does not get updated. I was able to work around it with:

```r
reactable::reactable(mtcars,
    theme = reactable::reactableTheme(
        color = "white",
        backgroundColor = "#1d1f21",
        stripedColor = "#4a4b4d"
    ),
    columns = list(
        carb = reactable::colDef(
            sticky = "right"
        )
    ),
    striped = TRUE,
    rowStyle = function(index) {
        if (index %% 2 == 1) {
            list(backgroundColor = "#4a4b4d")
        } else {
            list(backgroundColor = "#1d1f21")
        }
    }
)
```

which isn't quite perfect, but is close enough for the time being.

